[Music]  hi everybody and welcome back i'm james  powell  you may know me as co-chair of the num  focus board of directors  you may know me as vice president of pi  data with a purview over all  global pi data conferences and meetups  or you might know me as the guy at the  python conferences in the suit  i'm really excited to speak to aaron  richter a data scientist at saturn cloud  today  aaron's going to share with us a little  bit about how saturn cloud and early  sage startup  has invested in the pi data ecosystem  and the open source scientific computing  ecosystem  but i also want to share with you a  little detail i know one of the founders  of saturn cloud hugo  i've known hugo for a couple of years  now and i've known that  even before founding saturn cloud hugo  has had an investment  and an interest in supporting the open  source scientific computing ecosystem  the pi data ecosystem and the pi data  community so i'm  very excited to hear more from aaron  about what saturn cloud is up to  so aaron would you like to introduce  yourself to all of our pi data global  attendees  watching this from home sure hi my name  is aaron richter i'm a data scientist  data engineer started off as a software  developer and kind of  came into the data science world right  when the big data  wave was taking over so i did a lot of  distributed computing back in the day  i did a lot of machine learning and data  science  in the pi data ecosystem now i'm a  senior data scientist at saturn cloud  saturn cloud is an end-to-end  data science and machine learning  platform for data scientists and their  teams to take the most advantage of the  pi data ecosystem  and scale that on using das and rapids  so  my focus at saturn cloud is to ensure  that people know  how to use these tools most effectively  and i also help clients  kind of migrate some of their maybe  single node python workloads into  cluster computing  or gpu computing we're all about making  it really easy for people to accelerate  their workloads  abstracting away a lot of infrastructure  and hardware setup so that way data  scientists can focus on writing code  building models creating visualizations  and then creating value at their  companies  erin tell me a little bit about what  open source tools  especially tools from the pi data stack  that you all use at saturn cloud  yeah so we we do a lot in the open  source and  pi data community there's a couple  places where we use the tools  one in our product and then ways that we  enable our users  to take advantage of open source tools  so in the product  we use things like docker and kubernetes  for containerization  hosting all of our apps and we'll  utilize a lot of  the open source das and jupiter  components  because that's part of our product  offering and then for the web app we use  vue js what's your favorite open source  project my favorite open source project  in the like pi data community and really  focusing on the  accelerated computing space i would say  is rapids  i think that's really exciting because  it's pushing all of this data science  computation  onto the gpu and you see a lot of  performance increases and  i personally really believe that this is  the future of kind of accelerated  computing  and data science computing it just makes  sense to do your stuff on the gpus  because it's faster  saturn cloud also uses condo forge quite  heavily how does conda fit into this  environmental management  strategy yeah and so one of the one of  the ways that we support defining an  environment is through a conda  environment file so you can copy and  paste your yaml that you would normally  use  for like a conda and create and that's  typically the way that i really create a  lot of my environments and that we  recommend as well  because that way conda is doing the  solving it's figuring out which packages  are compatible it'll let you know if  there are incompatibilities and then  we'll kind of  take over that part you know once condo  has created that python environment  we'll make sure that that gets hooked up  into the docker  and that way you know all of your nodes  are now using the same  environment so i know saturn cloud is a  fairly young company  but has already made a name for itself  contributing to  the pi data community after all you're  one of our sponsors for pi data global  what motivates saturn cloud to be  a contributor to the community in this  way from the very birth of the company  yeah so from the beginning we have  always been  full on open source pi data community  it's really  part of our business success our entire  goal is to ensure that data scientists  and data science teams  can successfully use the pi data  ecosystem scale  their code with desks so we wanted to  make sure  that we were contributing from the  beginning because we're relying  and relying on top of these awesome  packages we want to make sure that we  can continue to contribute to them  by actually adding code to them  contributing to events and pushing  forward  the community because this is really  honestly it's central to our business  so not only is it makes sense  commercially for us because if these  open source packages are good people are  going to continue to use saturn  but also we truly believe in  these packages and in this community  because it has such a great impact  across  all businesses when we can do good data  science so from from those two aspects  that's why we've always been  kind of open source first from the  beginning so what would you say to a  colleague at a similarly sized  early stage startup to try to convince  them to  employ their limited resources to better  engagement with the community  yeah i would say from the beginning it's  open source is awesome because there are  so many different people working on  different problems you can usually find  an open source package that solves your  problem  but that all of the reason that that  open source package  exists is because somebody was  passionate about it someone was spending  their time  sometimes even their nights and weekends  on working on that package  you know we've we hear stories a lot of  some of the top  open source packages have three or five  core maintainers  who have kind of been doing this on the  side or on a volunteer basis so  if you think about all the money as a  business that you would spend  on on tools for you know email  and and managing all of your your  employees and  any other thing where you you would kind  of like spend money on a commercial  offering  you get this open source stuff for free  but  it still requires people to contribute  and people who create that so from the  beginning  it kind of just makes sense to  contribute there also  you want to make sure that this open  source tool that you might be depending  on  is going to stick around for the future  that there if bugs are going to come out  they can be fixed so first if you can  kind of  contribute to the package financially  you can know that there will be people  there funded to  to kind of work on those things and even  better if you can commit  an employee or resource on your team to  contributing directly to those packages  you now  know that it's gonna it's going to kind  of be stable you know if something goes  wrong somebody's right in there  talking to the maintainers working with  them and they can get it fixed that has  happened a lot for us at saturn is  you know i would run into a problem with  desk and then i would kind of talk to  some of my colleagues and they're like  oh yeah i saw that issue  just three days ago and there are these  people working on it and it really helps  a lot to be that well connected to a  community especially for packages that  you really count them  so let me ask you this if you were  interviewing for a new job  and the company asked you what excites  you most about this position  how would you try to convince them to  increase their contribution open source  or even start contributing to open  source how would you try and convince  them that it's worthwhile  for you as a new hire to be more  invested in open source  yeah i think especially thinking about  the being in the space of  kind of tools focused work  i would really want to convince any sort  of employer or my future employer  to commit to open source because first  of all  it creates this authority in the  community  so when when people see that your staff  members are actually contributing to  these open source packages that they're  using  when they're answering their questions  when they're fixing bugs it shows that  you as a company  actually know what you're doing are  investing  in this tool and then people who want to  buy whatever product or service  you're offering have a lot more trust in  what you're doing because you are  there at the core building kind of in  the weeds making sure everything's  working  so aaron pi data global is not the first  pi data  conference that you've attended outside  of just the software  pi data to a lot of people means a  community of like-minded users  what interests you or excites you or  engages you most about the pi data  community  pi data has been an awesome community  for me my  first exposure to it was through the pi  data miami group that's actually  relatively new and i was able to help  kind of build that up be part of the  organizing efforts  and do a conference here but i would say  that this paid community  probably is the the biggest community  thing that  even gave me my job at saturn i wouldn't  have known about saturn cloud but i  actually met saturn cloud  through pi data before i even started  working there so  for me personally it's been a great  avenue for my own career growth  i've been able to meet awesome people  that even people who have  created big open source packages and  these kind of  more celebrity names that you would hear  and see on twitter or  kind of see contributing to packages and  like wow you know those people are  really smart and doing awesome things  i'm able to either sit in the same room  with them meet them talk to them see  them at different conferences  virtually currently um but i think the  pi data community has been really great  for this entire ecosystem of tools but  even just as a data scientist trying to  kind of progress my career meet people  network  it's it's been an awesome community if  you knew somebody who is interested in  applying to work at saturn cloud  what might you tell them to take that  from merely interested to  really excited i'd say if you want to  be able to contribute to the future  of data science and the future of  accelerated computing  you should apply to work at saturn cloud  we're on the cutting edge  of a lot of tools that i really believe  are going to change the way that people  interact with data and compute with data  so you should definitely check us out  so in addition to contributing back to  the projects what else does saturn cloud  do  to contribute back to the community yeah  so saturn is really  committed to not only the code but also  to the community of people  that are learning all of these tools so  we especially in  these covent times all the meetups have  gone virtual so we've helped to  host and sponsor some pi data meetups  particularly with the new york city  miami and philadelphia groups and we've  been working on helping  getting speakers helping getting  software and making sure that those  communities can continue to kind of keep  moving forward as well so  i'm excited to participate in those and  make sure that  these community meetups can still keep  going forward and that we can actually  talk to some people in different cities  that we might normally have not done  before  so aaron thank you for joining us today  i really enjoyed hearing more about how  saturn cloud has invested in the das  ecosystem and i'm really looking forward  to seeing your talk at pi data global  thank you very much i really enjoyed  this i'm really looking forward to  interacting with everybody at the pi  data global conference  [Music]  you

